This is the exercism forth exercise. The `Forth` function takes `[]string` (each element is a line of input) and returns `([]int, error)`.

Key semantics:
- Each input line is a program line.
- Words: numbers (sequences of digits, possibly with a leading `-`), and words (letters, digits, symbols, punctuation that aren't numbers).
- Words are case-insensitive.
- Definition: `: word-name definition ;`
- Built-in words: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `dup`, `drop`, `swap`, `over`.
- Division by zero is an error.
- Stack underflow is an error.
- Unknown word is an error.
- Defining a word with the name of a number is an error ("cannot redefine a number").
- "Can define a word that uses a word with the same name": `: foo 10 ;` followed by `: foo foo 1 + ;` — when defining the second `foo`, the `foo` inside the definition refers to the old `foo` (the one defined first). So when parsing a definition, word references inside the definition must be resolved at definition time (immediately), i.e., the body of the definition is expanded to tokens that resolve existing words at definition time.

Wait, but "can use different words with the same name": `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;`, `: foo 6 ;`, `bar foo` → `5 6`. When `bar` is defined, `foo` refers to the first definition (push 5). Then `foo` is redefined to push 6. Executing `bar foo` → bar pushes 5, foo pushes 6. This works with immediate resolution at definition time: bar's body is resolved to [push 5] at definition time.

And `: foo 10 ;` `: foo foo 1 + ;` `foo` → 11. When the second `foo` is defined, the `foo` in the body resolves to the first `foo` (push 10). So the body becomes [push 10, push 1, +]. Executing foo → 11. Good — immediate resolution at definition time.

How to represent a word definition? For simplicity, we can represent the definition body as a list of "actions" — each token in the body resolves to either a number push or a function. But note: when a user-defined word references another user-defined word, and that word is later redefined, the reference should retain the old meaning (per the test case). So we should resolve tokens to concrete actions at definition time.

